---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import Testimonials from '../components/Testimonials.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import { faqSchema, serviceSchema } from '../data/schema';
import session from '../assets/photos/strengths-session.jpg';
import presenting from '../assets/photos/lyncia-presenting.jpg';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is CliftonStrengths?', a: "It's an online assessment developed by Gallup, the research organisation best known for its polls on workplace engagement and wellbeing. It identifies your natural patterns of thinking, feeling and behaving across 34 'themes', and ranks them so you can see what comes most naturally to you." },
  { q: 'Our team has already done the assessment. Can you still help?', a: 'Yes. Plenty of teams have done the assessment and then filed the report away. We can pick up from wherever you are and put the results to work.' },
  { q: 'How long is a team workshop?', a: "Whatever suits you. Half a day, a full day, or a series of shorter sessions. I'll find out what you need first, then build the session around that." },
  { q: 'Can you run sessions outside Wellington?', a: "Yes. I've run strengths work with teams in Auckland and elsewhere, and individual debriefs work well online." },
  // TODO(Lyncia): confirm how assessment codes are handled and whether their cost is included.
  { q: 'Is the assessment included?', a: "I can arrange the assessment for you or your team as part of the work. We'll cover the details when we plan your session." },
];

const description = 'Gallup CliftonStrengths coaching and team workshops in Wellington and online with a Gallup Certified Strengths Coach. Help people understand themselves and work better together.';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Gallup CliftonStrengths Coaching & Team Workshops, Wellington NZ"
  description={description}
  schema={[serviceSchema('Gallup CliftonStrengths coaching and team workshops', description, '/strengths'), faqSchema(faqs)]}
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Gallup CliftonStrengths"
    title="Start with what's right with you"
    lead="What would happen if we stopped fixating on what's wrong with people and focused on what's right with them? As a Gallup Certified Strengths Coach, I help individuals and teams understand what they naturally bring, and how to use it."
    image={session}
    imageAlt="Lyncia talking through a CliftonStrengths report with a client"
  />

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="container-page grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Why strengths?</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-lg text-muted">
          <p>
            Most of us know our weaknesses very well. Social media, performance reviews and our own inner critic are happy
            to point them out. Very few of us have a clear picture of what we're naturally good at.
          </p>
          <p>
            The strengths approach came out of Gallup's research. Instead of asking "what's broken and how do we fix it?",
            it asks "how do we get the best out of what this person most naturally brings?"
          </p>
          <p>
            Knowing your strengths isn't about copying someone else. It's okay to want the outcomes another person has. You
            don't need to <em>be</em> them to get there.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-3xl bg-navy p-8 text-white sm:p-10">
        <p class="eyebrow text-mint">Gallup's research</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-white/80">People who use their strengths every day are:</p>
        <dl class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div><dt class="font-display text-4xl text-white">3×</dt><dd class="mt-1 text-white/80">as likely to report an excellent quality of life</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-display text-4xl text-white">6×</dt><dd class="mt-1 text-white/80">as likely to be engaged in their jobs</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-display text-4xl text-white">7.8%</dt><dd class="mt-1 text-white/80">more productive</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-white/60">Source: <a class="text-white/80" href="https://www.gallup.com/cliftonstrengths/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Gallup CliftonStrengths</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-sand/60 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="container-page max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">The part people don't expect</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Your strengths are also where you trip up</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-lg text-muted">
        <p>
          The themes at the top of your profile are the things you're good at and enjoy. They're also where your blind
          spots tend to be.
        </p>
        <p>
          Take someone with <strong class="text-navy">Learner</strong> high on their profile. They love researching and
          they're good at it. But they can get so absorbed in gathering information that the job never gets finished.
          Knowing that about yourself means you can notice when you've got enough to make a decision, and move on.
        </p>
        <p>
          Or the colleague who always points out what won't work first. They aren't being negative. They see the risks
          straight away. Put that in the right place, like project planning, and it's a real asset.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="container-page grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <article class="rounded-3xl border border-navy/10 bg-white p-8 sm:p-10">
        <p class="eyebrow">For individuals</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl">Understand yourself better</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
          You complete the assessment, then we go through your results together. That's where the useful insights come
          from. We look at what energises you, how your strengths show up at work and at home, and where they might be
          getting in your way. Strengths often form the starting point for ongoing coaching.
        </p>
        <a href="/coaching" class="btn-secondary mt-6">About coaching</a>
      </article>
      <article class="rounded-3xl border border-navy/10 bg-white p-8 sm:p-10">
        <p class="eyebrow">For teams</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl">Understand each other better</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
          A team that understands each other's strengths knows what everyone has to offer, what motivates them, what they
          might struggle with, and how to support each other. I tailor every team session to the challenge you're facing,
          and to how much your people already know about strengths.
        </p>
        <a href="/for-organisations" class="btn-secondary mt-6">Working with organisations</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-mint-soft py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="container-page grid items-center gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <Image src={presenting} alt="Lyncia facilitating a group session" widths={[500, 800]} sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 540px, 90vw" class="aspect-[4/3] w-full rounded-3xl object-cover" />
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">An example</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl">Helping managers and new graduates work together</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-muted">
          At one professional services firm, new graduates report directly to managers who are often only a few years
          ahead of them. We sat each pair down with both of their strengths profiles.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted">
          The manager learned how to explain things so their graduate really understood. The graduate learned how their
          own strengths could help, and where they might get in the way. And they worked out simple things, like how to ask
          for help. One manager with Adaptability high on their profile was happy to be interrupted any time. Another,
          with Focus near the top, needed questions saved up for set times. Small insight, big difference.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Testimonials service="strengths" heading="From a team I've worked with" />
  <FAQ items={faqs} />
  <CTA heading="Let's talk about your team" text="Tell me what's going on and what you'd like to be different. I'll suggest an approach that fits, whether that's a half-day session or something longer." />
</BaseLayout>
